"[PDF p23]\nFundamentals of Command and Control\n07 July 2026 ADP 6-0 13\n1-71. Mission command requires commanders to issue mission orders. Mission orders are directives that\nemphasize to subordinates the results to be attained, not how they are to achieve them. Mission orders\nenable subordinates to understand the situation, their commander's mission and intent, and their own tasks.\nSubordinate commanders decide how to accomplish their own mission. The commander's intent and concept\nof operations set guidelines that provide unity of effort while allowing subordinate commanders to exercise\ninitiative in planning, preparing, and executing their operations.\nNote. A mission order is not a separate type of order. It is a technique for writing orders that allows\nsubordinates maximum freedom of action in accomplishing missions.\n1-72. Staffs prepare clear, concise orders by using doctrinally correct military terms and symbols. Using the\ncorrect terms and symbols minimizes chances of misunderstanding and aids with brevity. Developing shorter\nplans helps maintain simplicity. Shorter plans are easier to disseminate, read, and remember.\n1-73. Mission orders succinctly state the mission, task organization, commander's intent and concept of\noperations, tasks to subordinate units, and essential coordinating instructions. Tasks to subordinate units\ninclude all the standard elements (who, what, when, where, and why) with particular emphasis on the purpose\n(why). Mission orders are not an extended list of every task a unit may be required to accomplish. It never\nrepeats items that are part of the standard operating procedures or that are commonly understood by the force.\nMission orders do not contain directives to a subordinate that dictate how a task is to be accomplished. That\nis the province of the subordinate.\n1-74. The proper level of detail in a mission order is situationally\ndependent. Some phases of operations require tighter control\nover subordinate elements than other phases. An air assault's air\nmovement and landing phases, for example, require precise\nsynchronization. Its ground maneuver plan requires less detail.\nAs a rule, the base plan or order contains only the information\nrequired to synchronize combat power at the decisive time and\nplace while allowing subordinates as much freedom of action as possible. Commanders rely on subordinates'\ninitiative and coordination to act within the commander's intent and concept of operations.\n1-75. Graphics that accompany mission orders are drawn in a manner that allows maximum flexibility during\nexecution. They provide enough control for those activities requiring synchronization, but they avoid\nconstraining subordinates' freedom of action within their assigned area. Ideally, graphics provide enough\nreferences such as checkpoints and phase lines for subordinate leaders to use them as a basis of shared\nunderstanding when deviation from the base order becomes necessary. Flexible graphics and mission orders\ntogether create conditions for initiative and rapid decision making.\n1-76. Using mission orders does not mean commanders do not supervise subordinates during operations.\nSubordinates are accountable to their commanders for the use of delegated authority, but commanders remain\nsolely responsible and accountable for the actions over which subordinates exercise delegated authority.\nThus, commanders have the responsibility to check on their subordinates and provide directions and guidance\nas required to focus their activities. Commanders emphasize mission orders during training when actual\nconsequences are low, allowing subordinates to develop their own solutions to problems and intervening only\nwhen necessary. This is valuable both for subordinates to gain experience in problem solving and confidence\nin exercising initiative and for commanders to develop an understanding of the capabilities of subordinates.\nDISCIPLINED I NITIATIVE\n1-77. Disciplined initiative refers to the duty individual subordinates have to exercise initiative within the\nconstraints of the commander's intent to achieve the desired end state. Simply put, disciplined initiative is\nwhen subordinates have the discipline to follow their orders and adhere to the plan until they realize that their\norders and the plan are no longer suitable for the situation in which they find themselves. This may occur\nbecause enemy forces do something unforeseen, there is a new or more serious threat, or a golden opportunity\nemerges that offers a greater chance of success than the original course of action. The subordinate leader then\ntakes action on their own initiative to adjust to the new situation and achieve their commander's intent,\nreporting to the commander about the new situation when able to do so.\nMission orders contain the proper\nlevel of detail in the context of a\nparticular situation. They are neither\nso detailed that they stifle initiative\nnor so general that they provide\ninsufficient direction.\n[PDF p24]\nChapter 1\n14 ADP 6-0 07 July 2026\n1-78. Leaders and subordinates who exercise disciplined initiative create opportunities by taking action to\ndevelop a situation without asking for further guidance. Commanders rely on subordinates to act to meet their\nintent, not simply adhere to a plan that is no longer working. Under mission command, subordinates are\nrequired to exercise disciplined initiative in the absence of orders, when current orders no longer apply, or\nwhen an opportunity or threat presents itself. They, on their own responsibility, take actions that they think\nwill best accomplish the mission when communication with higher echelons is intermittent or decisions must\nbe made immediately.\n1-79. A subordinate's initiative may be the starting point for seizing, retaining, and exploiting the operational\ninitiative by forcing an enemy to respond to friendly action. Operational initiative is the setting of tempo and\nterms of action throughout an operation (ADP 3-0). The effect of multiple subordinates exercising disciplined\ninitiative sets the conditions for friendly forces to seize the operational initiative in chaotic and ambiguous\nsituations.\n1-80. When exercising initiative, neither commanders nor subordinates are independent actors. Subordinates\nconsider at least the following two factors when deciding when to exercise initiative:\n• Whether the benefits of the action outweigh the risk of desynchronizing the overall operation.\n• Whether the action will further the higher commander's intent.\n1-81. The main consideration in exercising initiative is the urgency of the situation. If time permits,\nsubordinates attempt to communicate their new situation and recommended course of action to their\ncommander. Their commander then assesses the implications for the overall force, the impact on other\noperations, and whether subordinates require additional support. However, subordinates must depart from\ntheir orders when they are unable to contact their commander or when there is a limited amount of time to\nseize a fleeting opportunity. If doubt exists about whether to contact their commander or depart from orders\nand act to seize a fleeting opportunity, subordinates should act if they can do so within their commander's\nintent.\n1-82. Fostering a command climate that encourages disciplined initiative requires commanders to accept risk\nand underwrite the good faith mistakes of subordinates in training, before the unit is committed to combat.\nCommanders set conditions for subordinates to learn and gain the experience they need to operate on their\nown. Subordinates learn to trust that they have the authority and responsibility to act, knowing their\ncommander will back their decisions. Because mutual trust and shared understanding constitute the\nfoundation of subordinate initiative, commanders train subordinates to act within the commander's intent in\nuncertain situations.\nRISK ACCEPTANCE\n1-83. Risk is the probability and severity driven chance of\nloss, caused by threat or other hazards (ATP 5-19).\nCommanders account for risk when evaluating the likelihood\nof mission success. Risk can be assessed through the cost\nimposed by, or the impact on, achievement of objectives.\n1-84. Commanders analyze risk in collaboration with\nsubordinates to determine what level of risk exists and how to\nmitigate it. When considering how much risk to accept with a\ncourse of action, commanders consider risk to the mission and\nrisk to the force against the perceived benefit. They apply judgment regarding the importance of an objective,\ntime available, and anticipated cost. Commanders balance the tension between protecting the force and\naccepting risks that must be taken to accomplish their mission.\n1-85. The greatest opportunity may come from the course of action with the most risk. An example of this\nwould be committing significant forces to a potentially costly attack to fix the bulk of enemy forces in place\nto set the conditions for their envelopment by other forces. Another would be taking a difficult but unexpected\nroute to achieve surprise.\n1-86. While each situation is different, commanders avoid undue caution or commitment of resources to\nguard against every perceived threat. Expecting to avoid all risk is unrealistic and detrimental to mission\naccomplishment. Waiting for perfect intelligence and synchronization may increase risk or close a window\nThere is no magic formula for\nquantification of risk. Staffs provide\ndecision makers an assessment of the\nexpected risks, costs, benefits, and\npotential consequences of proposed\nmilitary actions. This facilitates decision\nmakers to reduce, control, or accept\nrisk.\n[PDF p25]\nFundamentals of Command and Control\n07 July 2026 ADP 6-0 15\nof opportunity. Mission command requires that commanders and subordinates manage accepted risk, exercise\ninitiative, and act decisively, even when the outcome is uncertain.\n1-87. Reasonably estimating and intentionally accepting risk is not gambling. Gambling occurs when the\ncommander risks the force without a reasonable level of information about the outcome. Therefore,\ncommanders avoid gambles. Commanders carefully determine risks, analyze and minimize as many hazards\nas possible, and then accept risk to accomplish the mission. (Refer to ATP 5-19 for more information on the\nrisk management process.)\nTHE ROLE OF SUBORDINATES IN MISSION COMMAND\n1-88. The mission command approach to C2 requires active participation by personnel of all ranks and duty\npositions. Subordinate officers, noncommissioned officers, and Soldiers all have important roles in the\nexercise of mission command. During operations, subordinates are delegated authority through orders and\nstandard operating procedures to make decisions within their commander's intent. Commanders expect\nsubordinates to exercise this authority to further the commander's intent when changes in the situation render\norders irrelevant, or when communications are lost with higher echelon headquarters.\n[PDF p26]\nChapter 1\n16 ADP 6-0 07 July 2026\nCorporal Alvin York and Mission Command\nOn the morning of 8 October 1918, Soldiers of the 82nd Division began an attack to\nsever the German supply network in the Argonne Forest. Among the men in this push\nwas Corporal Alvin York, a squad leader in the 328th Infantry Regiment.\nInitially, the American attack seemed to go well as forward-deployed Germans\nseemingly retreated in the face of superior numbers. However, this was a ruse, as the\nGermans were falling back into prepared positions. Once the Americans were in the\nmiddle of the kill zone, the Germans opened fire. This was quickly followed by German\nartillery ripping gaping holes in the American line.\nAmong the first to fall was Corporal York's platoon leader, Lieutenant Kirby Stewart,\nand as the casualties mounted, the American attack quickly foundered. With\nLieutenant Stewart dead, Sergeant Harry Parsons assumed command of Corporal\nYork's platoon. After surveying the situation, he ordered Sergeant Bernard Early,\nCorporal York, Corporal Murray Savage, and Corporal William Cutting to advance with\ntheir squads to a defile to the south. From here, Sergeant Parsons surmised that they\nmight be able to get behind the German lines and eliminate the machine guns that\nwere holding up the advance.\nAfter dodging German fire, Sergeant Early led his 16 men to the defilade, then up a cut\nin the valley that led behind the German positions. They slowly worked their way\naround the German infantry and subsequently surprised and captured some 70\nGerman soldiers, including the battalion commander. While the Americans tried to line\nup their prisoners, a machine-gun crew on a nearby hill yelled to the captured Germans\nto take cover and then opened fire. The blast of bullets killed six Americans and\nwounded three. Corporal York was the highest-ranking Soldier not hit, and he took\ncharge of the remaining seven men.\nWith the surviving Americans and German prisoners clinging to the ground, Corporal\nYork seized the initiative. He charged up the hill, outflanked the German machine gun\nand an infantry platoon, killing 19. Seeing a large group of German reinforcements\narriving from further up the hill, Corporal York went back to his men. As he came down\nthe hill, he was spotted by a German officer who ordered a bayonet charge to kill the\nAmerican. Seeing a platoon of Germans charging, Corporal York slid on his side, pulled\nout his 1911 Colt automatic pistol, and began picking off enemy soldiers from back to\nfront. Seeing this, the previously captured German battalion commander slowly got up\noff of the ground and approached Corporal York. Standing behind Corporal York, he\ncautiously yelled above the din, \"English?\" Corporal York replied, \"American!\" In\nexasperation, the German commander answered, \"Good Lord! If you won't shoot\nanymore, I will make them give up.”\nCorporal York and his men quickly organized their prisoners, which now numbered\n100, into a formation and began marching them out of the forest. During the march\nback to the American lines, the Americans ended up walking into another group of\nGermans. Corporal York secured their surrender as well, increasing his prisoner count\nto 132. This saved his unit from destruction, thwarted a German counterattack, and\nallowed the 82nd Division to achieve its objective. For his heroism, Corporal York was\npromoted to sergeant, awarded the Medal of Honor, and would go down in history as\nAmerica's most celebrated hero of the First World War.\n[PDF p27]\nFundamentals of Command and Control\n07 July 2026 ADP 6-0 17\n1-89. Because mission command decentralizes decision making authority and grants subordinates significant\nfreedom of action, it demands more of subordinates at all levels. Commanders train and educate subordinates\nso that they demonstrate good judgment when exercising initiative. Subordinates are competent in their\nrespective fields, and they are confident they will have the commander's support to make and implement\ndecisions. They embrace opportunities to assume responsibility for achieving the commander's intent.\n1-90. Subordinates do not wait for a breakdown in communications or a crisis to learn how to act within the\ncommander's intent. Subordinates look for every opportunity to demonstrate and exercise initiative. To the\ngreatest extent possible, they report what they intend to do and then execute unless their commander\nspecifically denies them permission. As subordinates realize their commander will support sound decisions,\ntheir trust in the Commander increases and they become more willing to exercise initiative. As commanders\nsee subordinates perform in uncertain situations, they gain trust in their subordinates' judgment and ability.\nCOMMAND AND CONTROL WARFIGHTING FUNCTION\nOVERVIEW\n1-91. A warfighting function is a group of tasks and systems united by a common purpose that commanders\nuse to accomplish missions and training objectives (ADP 3-0). The purpose of warfighting functions is to\nprovide an intellectual organization for common critical capabilities available to commanders and staffs at\nall echelons. The six Army warfighting functions are C2, movement and maneuver, intelligence, fires,\nsustainment, and protection. Each warfighting function complements and reinforces the other functions.\nTheir integration and synchronization generate the combat power available for commanders to execute\noperations and accomplish missions. (Refer to ADP 3-0 for more information on the warfighting functions.)\n1-92. The command and control warfighting function is the related tasks and a system that enable\ncommanders to exercise authority and direction to accomplish missions (ADP 3-0). The exercise of authority\nand direction includes integrating forces and synchronizing the warfighting functions effectively into a\nconcept of operations to achieve objectives and accomplish missions. The C2 warfighting function consists\nof the C2 warfighting function tasks and the C2 system.\n1-93. The C2 warfighting function tasks focus on integrating the activities of the other warfighting functions\nto accomplish missions. Commanders, assisted by their staffs, integrate numerous processes and activities\nwithin their headquarters and across the force through the C2 warfighting function tasks. These tasks include\nthe following:\n• Command forces (See chapter 2).\n• Control operations (See chapter 3).\n• Organize and employ the C2 system (See chapter 4).\n1-94. Commanders need support to effectively exercise C2. At\nevery echelon of command, each commander employs a C2\nsystem: the arrangement of people, processes, data and networks,\nand C2 nodes that enable commanders to conduct operations. The\nC2 system supports the commander's decision making,\ndisseminates the commander's decisions to subordinates, and\nfacilitates controlling forces. Commanders employ their C2 system to enable the people and formations\nconducting operations to work towards a common purpose. All equipment and procedures exist to achieve\nthis end. Commanders organize the four components of their C2 system to support decision making and\nfacilitate communication. The C2 system enables commanders to communicate with higher, adjacent,\nsupporting, and subordinate commands to control all aspects of operations. (See chapter 4 for a detailed\ndiscussion of the C2 system.)\nCommanders seek to improve their\nC2 system for operational\nadvantage and adapt the system\nbased on changing circumstances.\n[PDF p28]\nThis page intentionally left blank."